Z||000000||ArchMagi||02-21-2001||10:33 PM||||Can anyone give me some info on the 86 grand am's? and mainly, what type of engine does it have? i herd its the same 4cyl as our fieros have.  any truth to this?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||danny boy||02-21-2001||11:18 PM|||| Yep, the very same. I think they came vith 2.8's too, but I'm not sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-22-2001||12:03 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by danny boy:<BR><B> Yep, the very same. I think they came vith 2.8's too, but I'm not sure.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>No, the V-6 in the early Grand Am's was a Buick 3.0L engine. (Only used for 3 years then they used the quad4 and later 3.1L)<BR>The engine had a lot of reliability issues.<BR>(I had an 85 grand am. Got tired of the weekly drive to the dealer to fix it. Traded it back for an 86 Fiero SE!!!)<BR>The  4-cyl's were 2.5L dukes. up until 1988 or 1989 then it was the quad4's.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||02-22-2001||01:46 AM||||My sisters Grand Am (91) has a different block and head than other cars. (Still a 2.5 duke) If the car has a U as the engine code on the vin, stay away.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-22-2001||08:38 PM||||87 model year and after, all 2.5's are the DIS ignition and 700 TBI. You can't use these to replace the older distributor motors. You can't use most parts off these on the older motors either.||||reg||1
